<p>A regular clean is the routine upkeep that keeps a home tidy and hygienic from week to week: hoovering, mopping, dusting, wiping kitchen worktops and cleaning the bathroom. A deep clean is far more thorough, reaching the grime a regular clean skips, such as limescale, greasy cupboard doors, skirting boards, the space behind appliances and the inside of fittings.</p>
<p>Put simply, a regular clean maintains a home and a deep clean resets it. Most households get the best results from both: a deep clean to bring everything up to standard, then regular visits to keep it there.</p>
<p>Below we compare a deep clean vs a regular clean in detail, covering what each includes, how long they take and how to decide which one your home in Hull or East Yorkshire needs right now.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>A regular clean covers visible surfaces and everyday mess; a deep clean tackles built-up dirt in the places that usually get missed.</li><li>Deep cleans take considerably longer and often need more than one cleaner.</li><li>If a home hasn't been thoroughly cleaned for a while, start with a deep clean, then switch to regular cleaning.</li><li>Most homes benefit from a deep clean a few times a year, even with a weekly or fortnightly cleaner.</li></ul></div>
<h2>What is a regular clean?</h2>
<p>A regular clean is a repeat visit, usually weekly or fortnightly, that keeps on top of the everyday dirt a busy household creates. The focus is on the surfaces you see and use every day, so the home feels fresh and stays hygienic.</p>
<p>A typical <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular house cleaning</a> visit includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Hoovering carpets and rugs, and mopping hard floors</li>
<li>Dusting reachable surfaces, shelves and ornaments</li>
<li>Cleaning and disinfecting the toilet, basin, bath and shower</li>
<li>Wiping kitchen worktops, the hob, the sink and the outside of appliances</li>
<li>Emptying bins and general tidying</li>
<li>Making beds or changing bedding if requested</li>
</ul>
<p>Because a regular cleaner visits the same home week after week, they get to know how you like things done and which rooms need the most attention. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:what-does-a-regular-cleaner-do}}">what a regular cleaner does (and doesn't do)</a> covers this in more detail.</p>
<h2>What is a deep clean?</h2>
<p>A deep clean is a top-to-bottom clean that deals with build-up rather than day-to-day mess. It covers everything in a regular clean, then goes into the corners, edges and fittings that slowly collect dust, grease and limescale over months.</p>
<p>Our <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep cleaning service</a> typically adds tasks like these:</p>
<h3>Kitchen</h3>
<ul>
<li>Degreasing cupboard doors, handles and kickboards</li>
<li>Cleaning inside the microwave and fridge, and wiping appliance door seals</li>
<li>Scrubbing tiles, grout and splashbacks, and descaling taps</li>
<li>Cleaning the extractor hood and filters</li>
</ul>
<h3>Bathroom</h3>
<ul>
<li>Removing limescale from taps, shower heads, screens and tiles</li>
<li>Scrubbing grout and silicone, and cleaning behind and around the toilet base</li>
<li>Wiping extractor fan covers, radiators, towel rails and pipework</li>
</ul>
<h3>Living areas and bedrooms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Skirting boards, door frames, doors and light switches</li>
<li>High-level dusting of cornicing, light fittings and the tops of wardrobes</li>
<li>Interior windows, sills and frames</li>
<li>Hoovering under and behind furniture where it can be moved safely</li>
</ul>
<p>For the full detail, see our <a href="{{guide:kitchen-deep-clean-checklist}}">kitchen deep clean checklist</a> and our step-by-step guide to <a href="{{guide:bathroom-deep-clean-guide}}">deep cleaning a bathroom</a>.</p>
<h2>Deep clean vs regular clean: side-by-side comparison</h2>
<p>The table below sums up the main differences between the two types of clean.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Regular clean</th><th>Deep clean</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Maintain a clean home</td><td>Remove built-up dirt and reset the home</td></tr>
<tr><td>How often</td><td>Weekly or fortnightly</td><td>A few times a year, or as a one-off</td></tr>
<tr><td>Focus</td><td>Visible, everyday surfaces</td><td>Hidden areas, edges, fittings and build-up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Limescale and grease</td><td>Light surface wipe</td><td>Properly descaled and degreased</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skirting boards, doors and switches</td><td>Occasionally, on rotation</td><td>Every one, every time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time on site</td><td>A few hours</td><td>Often a full day, sometimes with a team</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Busy households staying on top of things</td><td>First cleans, moves, seasonal resets and homes that have slipped</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>When do you need a deep clean instead of a regular clean?</h2>
<p>A regular clean can only maintain a standard that's already there. If a home has slipped, weekly visits will spend months chipping away at the backlog. These are the situations where a deep clean makes more sense:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Before starting regular cleaning.</strong> Many people book a deep clean first so their regular cleaner starts from a clean baseline.</li>
<li><strong>Moving in or out.</strong> Empty rooms show every mark. A <a href="{{service:move-in-cleaning}}">move-in clean</a> means you unpack into a home that's genuinely clean.</li>
<li><strong>Seasonal resets.</strong> Spring and the run-up to Christmas are popular times to clear winter grime or get ready for guests.</li>
<li><strong>After illness in the house.</strong> A thorough clean of touch points, bathrooms and soft furnishings helps the home feel fresh again.</li>
<li><strong>When life has got on top of you.</strong> A new baby, a long-term illness, a demanding job or caring responsibilities can all push cleaning down the list. There's no judgement; that's exactly what deep cleans are for.</li>
<li><strong>Older homes with condensation.</strong> Terraces and older semis often need regular attention to mould on window frames and bathroom ceilings.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you're unsure how often your home needs this level of attention, our guide on <a href="{{guide:how-often-should-you-deep-clean}}">how often you should deep clean</a> breaks it down by household.</p>
<h2>How long does a deep clean take compared with a regular clean?</h2>
<p>A regular clean of an average family home usually takes a few hours. A deep clean of the same home takes considerably longer, often a full day for one cleaner or a shorter visit from a small team.</p>
<p>The exact time depends on:</p>
<ul>
<li>The size of the property and the number of bathrooms</li>
<li>How long it's been since the last thorough clean</li>
<li>How much limescale, grease or mould there is</li>
<li>Pets, which add hair and odour work</li>
<li>Extras such as oven, carpet or upholstery cleaning</li>
</ul>
<p>That's why we give a fixed quote, agreed before work starts, rather than leaving you guessing how many hours a job will run to.</p>
<h2>Can a regular cleaner do a deep clean?</h2>
<p>Yes, but not within the time of a normal visit. A cleaner booked for a set number of hours each week will prioritise the essentials, so deep tasks like descaling a shower screen or degreasing every cupboard door tend to be done on rotation, a little at a time.</p>
<p>To keep things simple, book a separate deep clean once or twice a year and let your regular cleaner maintain it in between. Some jobs need specialist products and are better booked on their own. Professional oven cleaning, for example, uses fume-free, non-caustic products that are kinder to a family kitchen than many shop-bought oven cleaners.</p>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before your first professional clean, clear clutter from worktops and floors so the cleaner can spend their time cleaning rather than moving things. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:preparing-for-your-first-clean}}">preparing for your first professional clean</a> has a short checklist.</p></div>
<h2>Deep clean or regular clean: which should you book?</h2>
<p>Ask yourself one question: would a couple of hours of cleaning make the home feel properly clean? If yes, a regular clean is enough. If you'd still see limescale on the taps, dust on the skirting boards and grease on the cupboards afterwards, start with a deep clean.</p>
<p>For most homes the answer is a combination: a deep clean to start, regular cleaning every week or fortnight, and another deep clean every few months or with the seasons. Our <a href="{{guide:spring-cleaning-checklist}}">room-by-room spring cleaning checklist</a> is a good guide to what a seasonal deep clean should cover.</p>
<h2>Booking a deep clean in Hull and East Yorkshire</h2>
<p>Our team carries out deep cleans across the region, from Victorian terraces on the Avenues to family homes in <a href="{{area:beverley}}">Beverley</a> and the surrounding villages. Our cleaners are DBS-checked and insured, bring their own equipment and products, and eco-friendly products are available on request. If anything isn't right, we'll come back and re-clean it.</p>
<p>Find out more about <a href="{{local:deep-cleaning-hull}}">deep cleaning in Hull</a> or read about our full <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep cleaning service</a>. When you're ready, <a href="{{page:contact}}">get in touch</a> for a fixed quote, and we'll help you decide whether a deep clean, regular cleaning or a mix of both suits your home.</p>
