<p>The right frequency depends less on the calendar and more on how your home is used. A one-bedroom flat with a single occupant builds up dirt slowly; a busy family house with a dog and a muddy garden doesn't.</p>
<p>This guide gives a realistic deep cleaning schedule, task by task, and explains what changes it for homes across Hull and East Yorkshire, where hard water and condensation both play a part.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Aim for a whole-house deep clean every three to six months, and more often with pets, children or allergies.</li><li>Spread some deep cleaning tasks across the year so no single weekend becomes overwhelming.</li><li>Hard water and condensation in many local homes mean limescale and mould need attention more often.</li><li>A good weekly routine stretches the time between deep cleans but doesn't replace them.</li></ul></div>
<h2>How often should you deep clean your house? The short answer</h2>
<p>As a rule of thumb:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Every three months</strong> for homes with pets, young children, smokers, allergy or asthma sufferers, or several adults sharing</li>
<li><strong>Every four to six months</strong> for a typical household with a regular cleaning routine</li>
<li><strong>Once or twice a year</strong> for small homes with one or two occupants who keep on top of weekly cleaning</li>
</ul>
<p>On top of that, book a deep clean whenever there's a reason to reset: moving in, after building work, before hosting or after a period of illness. If you're not sure what separates this from routine upkeep, read our explanation of the <a href="{{guide:deep-clean-vs-regular-clean}}">difference between a deep clean and a regular clean</a>.</p>
<p>A professional <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> covers the whole house in one go, which is often easier than finding several free weekends.</p>
<h2>What affects how often you need a deep clean?</h2>
<h3>Pets</h3>
<p>Dogs and cats shed hair into carpets, sofas and skirting boards, and bring in mud and odours. Even with daily hoovering, hair works its way into places a quick clean won't reach. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:cleaning-with-pets}}">cleaning a home with pets</a> has tips for keeping on top of it between deep cleans.</p>
<h3>Children</h3>
<p>Sticky fingerprints on doors and switches, crumbs down the sofa and spills on carpets all add up quickly. Family homes generally need more frequent deep cleans of kitchens, living rooms and soft furnishings.</p>
<h3>Allergies and asthma</h3>
<p>Dust mites, pet dander and mould spores collect in mattresses, curtains and carpets. If someone in the house has allergies, regular deep cleaning of these areas can make a noticeable difference. See our guide on <a href="{{guide:cleaning-for-allergies}}">cleaning for allergies and asthma</a>.</p>
<h3>Hard water</h3>
<p>Much of Hull and East Yorkshire has hard water, so limescale builds up quickly on taps, shower screens and kettles. Our <a href="{{guide:hard-water-limescale-east-yorkshire}}">hard water and limescale guide</a> explains how to keep it under control.</p>
<h3>Older homes and condensation</h3>
<p>Victorian and Edwardian terraces, common across Hull and in villages like <a href="{{area:cottingham}}">Cottingham</a>, often have solid walls and cold spots where condensation settles. Black mould on window reveals and bathroom ceilings tends to return each winter, so these homes benefit from a deep clean as the heating season ends.</p>
<h2>A deep cleaning schedule, task by task</h2>
<p>Rather than doing everything at once, many people spread deep cleaning tasks out. Here's a realistic schedule.</p>
<h3>Every month</h3>
<ul>
<li>Descale taps, shower heads and the kettle</li>
<li>Clean the microwave inside and out</li>
<li>Wipe skirting boards, door frames and light switches</li>
<li>Run a hot maintenance wash on the washing machine and clean the door seal and drawer</li>
<li>Clean the dishwasher filter</li>
<li>Hoover mattresses and under beds</li>
</ul>
<h3>Every three months</h3>
<ul>
<li>Clean the oven, hob and extractor filters (more often if you cook a lot; see <a href="{{guide:how-often-clean-oven}}">how often to clean your oven</a>)</li>
<li>Pull out and clean behind the fridge, cooker and washing machine where it's safe to do so</li>
<li>Wipe kitchen cupboard doors and clean inside the fridge thoroughly</li>
<li>Deep clean bathroom grout and silicone</li>
<li>Wash cushion covers, throws and pillows (check the care labels)</li>
<li>Clean inside windows, frames and sills</li>
</ul>
<h3>Every six months</h3>
<ul>
<li>Empty and wipe inside kitchen cupboards and drawers</li>
<li>Wash curtains or have them cleaned, and dust blinds slat by slat</li>
<li>Dust light fittings, cornicing and the tops of wardrobes</li>
<li>Turn or rotate mattresses if the manufacturer recommends it</li>
<li>Defrost the freezer if it isn't frost-free</li>
</ul>
<h3>Every year</h3>
<ul>
<li>Have carpets and upholstery professionally cleaned</li>
<li>Wash walls and paintwork in high-traffic areas</li>
<li>Clear out the loft, garage and storage cupboards</li>
<li>Clean radiators, including between the fins and behind them</li>
</ul>
<p>Carpets in particular hold far more dirt than they show. Annual <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">carpet and upholstery cleaning</a> using hot water extraction lifts the grit and allergens a hoover leaves behind.</p>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Put deep cleaning jobs in your calendar the same way you'd book a boiler service. A reminder for the first weekend of each quarter is much easier to stick to than a vague plan.</p></div>
<h2>Signs your home needs a deep clean now</h2>
<ul>
<li>A crust of limescale on taps, or a cloudy shower screen that doesn't wipe clear</li>
<li>Black mould spots on silicone, window seals or ceilings</li>
<li>A greasy, sticky film on kitchen cupboards and the extractor hood</li>
<li>Smells that come back soon after cleaning</li>
<li>Dust along skirting boards and on top of door frames</li>
<li>Carpets that look flat, grey or patchy in walkways</li>
<li>Allergy symptoms that seem worse at home</li>
</ul>
<p>If you recognise several of these, a full deep clean will get you back to a standard that's easy to maintain.</p>
<h2>Does regular cleaning mean you can deep clean less often?</h2>
<p>Yes, to a point. A good weekly routine stops grime building up, so deep cleans are quicker and can be spaced further apart. Homes with a <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular cleaner</a> often find a deep clean two or three times a year is plenty.</p>
<p>What regular cleaning won't do is reach everything. Behind appliances, inside cupboards, high-level dust and heavy limescale tend to fall outside a standard visit. Pairing a <a href="{{guide:weekly-cleaning-schedule}}">realistic weekly cleaning schedule</a> with planned deep cleans is the easiest way to keep the whole home in good shape.</p>
<h2>Planning deep cleans around the year</h2>
<p>Timing deep cleans with the seasons makes them easier to remember and more useful:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spring:</strong> clear winter condensation, mould and dust once the heating goes off, wash curtains and open the windows.</li>
<li><strong>Summer:</strong> tackle carpets and upholstery while they can dry quickly with the windows open.</li>
<li><strong>Autumn:</strong> clean radiators and window frames before the heating comes on, and check for early signs of damp.</li>
<li><strong>Before Christmas:</strong> get the kitchen, oven and guest rooms ready for visitors.</li>
</ul>
<p>Holiday lets and rental properties work differently. They usually need a deep clean between longer stays or tenancies, as well as at the start and end of the season.</p>
<h2>Getting help with deep cleaning</h2>
