	[
		'slug'        => 'hard-water-limescale-east-yorkshire',
		'title'       => 'How to Remove Limescale: A Hard Water Guide for Hull & East Yorkshire',
		'seo_title'   => 'How to Remove Limescale: Hard Water Guide for Hull',
		'description' => 'How to remove limescale from taps, showers, kettles and toilets, why Hull and East Yorkshire water is so hard, and simple ways to stop it coming back.',
		'category'    => 'home-cleaning',
		'service'     => 'deep-cleaning',
		'related'     => [ 'bathroom-deep-clean-guide', 'kitchen-deep-clean-checklist', 'eco-friendly-cleaning-products' ],
		'image'       => 'chrome tap water',
		'excerpt'     => 'Safe, practical ways to remove limescale from taps, showers, kettles and toilets, plus how to stop it building up in a hard water area.',
		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>To remove limescale, use a mild acid such as white vinegar, lemon juice or citric acid. Apply it, leave it to soften the deposits, scrub gently with a non-scratch pad or old toothbrush, then rinse with clean water and dry. Heavy build-up may need a few rounds or a branded limescale remover.</p>
<p>Limescale is the chalky white crust left behind when hard water evaporates. Much of Hull and East Yorkshire has hard water, so if your taps, shower screen and kettle seem to scale up within days, you're not imagining it.</p>
<p>This guide explains why it happens, the safest way to remove limescale from every common surface, and the habits that stop it coming back.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Limescale is mineral build-up from hard water, and mild acids such as vinegar or citric acid dissolve it.</li><li>Never mix limescale removers with bleach, and avoid acids on natural stone and some special tap finishes.</li><li>Drying surfaces after use is the simplest way to prevent limescale.</li><li>Little and often beats an occasional heavy scrub, which risks scratching chrome and glass.</li></ul></div>
<h2>Why is the water so hard in Hull and East Yorkshire?</h2>
<p>Water becomes "hard" when it picks up dissolved minerals, mainly calcium and magnesium, as it moves through rock such as chalk and limestone. Much of East Yorkshire sits on chalk, including the Yorkshire Wolds, and Yorkshire Water supplies the area, so hard water is the norm across many local postcodes.</p>
<p>When hard water is heated or evaporates, those minerals are left behind as limescale. That's why it builds up fastest in kettles, on shower heads, around tap bases and on glass that's left to dry with droplets on it.</p>
<p>To check the hardness at your address, whether you live in <a href="{{area:hull}}">Hull</a>, <a href="{{area:driffield}}">Driffield</a> or anywhere in between, enter your postcode on your water supplier's website. Hardness can vary between postcodes, so it's worth checking rather than assuming.</p>
<h2>What removes limescale, and what to avoid</h2>
<p>Limescale dissolves in acid, so the right product does most of the work for you. The trick is giving it time, rather than scrubbing harder. If years of build-up have got the better of you, a professional <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> is often the quickest way to get back to a clean starting point.</p>
<h3>What works</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>White vinegar:</strong> cheap and effective on taps, shower heads and kettles. Dilute it for regular use, or use it neat on heavy build-up.</li>
<li><strong>Citric acid:</strong> sold as a powder to dissolve in warm water. It smells less than vinegar and is good for kettles and washing machines.</li>
<li><strong>Lemon juice:</strong> fine for light marks on taps.</li>
<li><strong>Branded limescale removers:</strong> faster on thick deposits. Follow the label, ventilate the room and wear gloves.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide to <a href="{{guide:eco-friendly-cleaning-products}}">eco-friendly cleaning products</a> covers which natural options are genuinely effective.</p>
<h3>What to avoid</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mixing products.</strong> Never mix acidic descalers or vinegar with bleach; the combination can release harmful chlorine gas.</li>
<li><strong>Acids on natural stone.</strong> Marble, limestone and travertine are etched by acid, so use a stone-safe cleaner instead.</li>
<li><strong>Abrasive pads on chrome and glass.</strong> Wire wool and scouring powders scratch, and scratches catch even more limescale.</li>
<li><strong>Special finishes.</strong> Brushed brass, gold-effect, matt black and some coated taps can be damaged by acids. Check the manufacturer's care guide and test a hidden spot first.</li>
<li><strong>Leaving acid on too long.</strong> Some enamel and older fittings can dull. Rinse thoroughly once the scale has softened.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How to remove limescale from taps and shower heads</h2>
<ol>
<li>Soak a cloth or a few sheets of kitchen roll in white vinegar or a citric acid solution.</li>
<li>Wrap it round the tap spout or base. For a shower head, fill a small plastic bag with the solution and secure it with an elastic band so the nozzles sit in the liquid.</li>
<li>Leave it for around an hour, or longer for heavy build-up, unless the manufacturer's guidance for your finish says otherwise.</li>
<li>Scrub gently with an old toothbrush, paying attention to the aerator and around the base.</li>
<li>Rinse with clean water and run the tap or shower to flush out loosened scale.</li>
<li>Dry and buff with a microfibre cloth.</li>
</ol>
<p>If your shower head has rubber nozzles, rubbing them with your thumb every week or so pushes out scale between deep cleans.</p>
<h2>Removing limescale from kettles, shower screens and toilets</h2>
<h3>Kettles</h3>
<p>Half-fill the kettle with water and a couple of tablespoons of citric acid, or an equal mix of vinegar and water, bring it to the boil, then leave it to stand. Empty and rinse, then boil a full kettle of fresh water once or twice and pour it away, so no taste is left behind. In a hard water area this is worth doing monthly.</p>
<h3>Shower screens and glass</h3>
<p>Spray with diluted vinegar or a limescale remover, leave for a few minutes, then wipe with a non-scratch sponge and rinse. For a cloudy screen, repeat over a few sessions rather than scrubbing hard. Finish with a squeegee and a dry microfibre cloth.</p>
<h3>Toilets</h3>
<p>Turn off the water supply and flush to lower the water level, then apply limescale remover or citric acid to the ring and under the rim. Leave it as long as the label allows, scrub with a toilet brush, then turn the water back on and flush. Stubborn rings may need two or three treatments.</p>
<p>For more on grout, silicone and glass, see our <a href="{{guide:bathroom-deep-clean-guide}}">guide to deep cleaning a bathroom</a>.</p>
<h2>Limescale in washing machines, dishwashers and kitchens</h2>
<p>Scale inside appliances makes them work harder and can leave deposits on clothes and glasses.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Washing machine:</strong> run an empty hot wash with a descaler or citric acid every month or two, then clean the door seal and detergent drawer.</li>
<li><strong>Dishwasher:</strong> keep the salt topped up and set the water hardness setting to suit your area; the manual explains how. Rinse aid helps prevent spotting on glasses.</li>
<li><strong>Kitchen sink and taps:</strong> descale weekly and dry after use. Stainless steel sinks can be descaled with diluted vinegar, then rinsed and buffed dry.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="{{guide:kitchen-deep-clean-checklist}}">kitchen deep clean checklist</a> covers the rest of the kitchen, from extractor filters to the backs of appliances.</p>
<h2>How to stop limescale building up</h2>
<ul>
<li>Squeegee the shower screen and tiles after every shower</li>
<li>Dry taps and the sink with a microfibre cloth after use</li>
<li>Keep a spray bottle of diluted vinegar in the bathroom for a quick weekly mist and wipe (not on natural stone)</li>
<li>Descale the kettle monthly</li>
<li>Use a filter jug for the water you put in the kettle</li>
<li>Consider a whole-house water softener. Installers usually leave an unsoftened tap in the kitchen for drinking water, so take advice before fitting one</li>
</ul>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Limescale is one of the first things letting agents spot at check-out. If you rent, keeping on top of it weekly saves a big job at the end of your tenancy; see <a href="{{guide:what-letting-agents-check-at-check-out}}">what letting agents check at check-out</a>.</p></div>
<h2>When to call in a professional</h2>
<p>Years of scale on shower screens, taps and tiles can take several rounds to shift safely, and it's easy to scratch glass or damage finishes by scrubbing too hard. Our cleaners deal with limescale every day across East Yorkshire and know which products are safe on which surfaces.</p>
<p>Limescale removal is part of every <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> we carry out, and it's a key part of our <a href="{{service:end-of-tenancy-cleaning}}">end of tenancy cleaning</a>, where clear taps and glass make a real difference at check-out. Holiday let owners on the coast face the same problem between guests, and our <a href="{{guide:holiday-let-changeover-checklist}}">holiday let changeover checklist</a> shows where descaling fits into each turnaround.</p>
<p>If you'd like your taps, glass and tiles back to clear and shining, <a href="{{page:contact}}">contact us</a> for a fixed quote.</p>
