<p>A realistic weekly cleaning schedule splits housework into short daily habits plus one focus job each day, so no single day takes more than about half an hour. Keep up the daily basics, rotate the bigger jobs through the week, and leave one day free to catch up.</p>
<p>The problem with most cleaning schedules is that they're written for people with unlimited time. If you're juggling work, school runs, shifts or a commute into Hull, you need a plan that survives a busy week, not a perfect one you abandon by Wednesday.</p>
<p>Below is a weekly schedule that works for most households, with tips to adapt it around children, pets and shared houses, and advice on when it's worth handing the bigger jobs to a <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular house cleaner</a>.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Spend around 15 minutes a day on the basics so mess never builds up.</li><li>Give each day one focus area instead of cleaning the whole house at once.</li><li>Keep one day free so a missed task can be caught up without guilt.</li><li>Add monthly and seasonal deep cleaning tasks on top of the weekly routine.</li><li>A regular cleaner can take the weekly focus jobs off your list entirely.</li></ul></div>
<h2>Daily cleaning tasks: the 15-minute basics</h2>
<p>These small jobs stop the house slipping. None takes long, and together they make the weekly tasks much quicker.</p>
<ul>
<li>Wash up or load the dishwasher, and wipe the worktops and hob after dinner</li>
<li>Wipe the bathroom basin and taps, and squeegee the shower screen</li>
<li>Do a quick ten-minute tidy of the main living area</li>
<li>Deal with post and paperwork as it arrives</li>
<li>Empty the kitchen bin when it's full</li>
<li>Open the windows for a few minutes each morning to let moisture out</li>
<li>Run a load of washing if needed, and put it away the same day</li>
</ul>
<p>Squeegeeing the shower is worth the extra minute in a hard water area like East Yorkshire, because it stops limescale before it starts.</p>
<h2>A weekly cleaning schedule, day by day</h2>
<p>Here's a simple plan. Swap the days around to suit your week; the point is that each day has one clear job.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Focus</th><th>Tasks</th><th>Rough time</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Monday</td><td>Kitchen</td><td>Wipe cupboard fronts and appliance doors, clean the microwave, clear out the fridge, mop the floor</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>Bathrooms</td><td>Clean the toilet, basin, bath and shower, descale taps, wipe mirrors, mop the floor, change towels</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>Dusting and surfaces</td><td>Dust every room top to bottom, wipe light switches and door handles</td><td>20 to 30 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thursday</td><td>Floors</td><td>Hoover carpets, rugs and stairs, and mop hard floors</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Friday</td><td>Bedrooms and laundry</td><td>Change the bedding, wash and put it away, tidy bedrooms</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Saturday</td><td>Catch-up plus one extra</td><td>Pick up anything missed, plus one rotating job from the monthly list</td><td>30 to 45 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sunday</td><td>Rest and reset</td><td>Daily basics only, plan meals and check the week ahead</td><td>10 minutes</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Put the bins out the evening before bin day and wipe the lids at the same time, so smells don't build up by the back door.</p>
<h2>How to fit a cleaning schedule around work, children and pets</h2>
<h3>Working full time or shifts</h3>
<p>Attach jobs to things you already do. Wipe the basin while you brush your teeth, clean the hob while the kettle boils and hoover while dinner is in the oven. If you work long shifts, move the focus jobs to your days off and stick to the basics on working days.</p>
<h3>Families with young children</h3>
<p>Give children simple jobs, like putting toys away, matching socks or wiping the table. A basket in each room for quick toy clear-ups saves time every evening.</p>
<h3>Homes with pets</h3>
<p>Add a quick hoover of the main walkways most days, wipe paws at the door and wash pet bedding weekly. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:cleaning-with-pets}}">cleaning with pets</a> covers hair, odours and muddy paws in more detail.</p>
<h3>Shared houses and flats</h3>
<p>Turn the schedule into a rota, with each person owning one focus area per week and rotating monthly. Agree the daily basics, especially washing up and bins, at the start.</p>
<h2>Monthly and seasonal jobs to add to your routine</h2>
<p>A weekly schedule keeps the house clean, but some tasks only need doing every few weeks. Use the Saturday slot to work through these, one at a time:</p>
<ul>
<li>Clean the oven and extractor filters</li>
<li>Descale the kettle, shower head and taps thoroughly</li>
<li>Wipe skirting boards, doors and door frames</li>
<li>Clean the inside of the windows and wipe the sills</li>
<li>Hoover the sofa and under the cushions</li>
<li>Clean the washing machine drawer and seal, and the dishwasher filter</li>
<li>Wash pillows and throws</li>
</ul>
<p>For bigger jobs, such as behind appliances, inside cupboards and carpets, see our guide on <a href="{{guide:how-often-should-you-deep-clean}}">how often you should deep clean</a>. Many households keep on top of the week themselves and book a professional <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> a few times a year. If the oven is the job that always gets skipped, our <a href="{{service:oven-cleaning}}">oven cleaning service</a> takes it off your list.</p>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep a small caddy of the products you use most, along with cloths and gloves, and carry it from room to room. You'll waste far less time hunting for things. For gentler options, see our guide to <a href="{{guide:eco-friendly-cleaning-products}}">eco-friendly cleaning products that actually work</a>.</p></div>
<h2>Why cleaning schedules fail, and how to make yours stick</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Too ambitious.</strong> A plan with an hour of jobs a day won't survive a busy week. Start small and add tasks once the habit sticks.</li>
<li><strong>No catch-up day.</strong> Life happens. Without a buffer, one missed day makes the whole plan feel like a failure.</li>
<li><strong>Everything on one person.</strong> Share the jobs out and write the rota down where everyone can see it.</li>
<li><strong>Clutter.</strong> Cleaning takes far longer when you have to move things first, so clear surfaces as part of the daily tidy.</li>
<li><strong>The wrong tools.</strong> Good microfibre cloths, a decent hoover and a squeegee make every job quicker.</li>
<li><strong>Starting from a dirty house.</strong> A schedule maintains a standard; it can't create one. If the house has got away from you, start with a one-off deep clean and use the schedule from there.</li>
</ul>
<h2>When a regular cleaner makes sense</h2>
<p>If the schedule keeps slipping, or you'd simply rather spend your evenings and weekends elsewhere, a regular cleaner can take on the weekly focus jobs and leave you with just the daily basics. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:what-does-a-regular-cleaner-do}}">what a regular cleaner does</a> explains what's usually covered, and <a href="{{guide:how-to-choose-a-cleaner}}">how to choose a cleaner you can trust</a> covers insurance, DBS checks and reviews.</p>
<p>Our <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular house cleaning</a> can be weekly or fortnightly, with DBS-checked, insured cleaners who bring their own equipment and products. Key holding is available if you're out at work. We cover <a href="{{local:regular-house-cleaning-hull}}">regular house cleaning in Hull</a> and nearby towns such as <a href="{{area:hessle}}">Hessle</a>. <a href="{{page:contact}}">Get in touch</a> for a fixed quote, and we'll help you work out a schedule that suits your household.</p>
